### What are NavigableMap and NavigableSet?

NavigableMap is an interface that extends SortedMap with navigation methods for finding the closest match to a given key. NavigableSet does the same for sets. The standard implementations are TreeMap and TreeSet, both backed by red-black trees.

These collections are useful when you need sorted data with efficient nearest-neighbour lookups, range queries, or priority-based retrieval.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.NavigableMap;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class NavigableMapBasicDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NavigableMap<String, BigDecimal> accounts = new TreeMap<>();
        accounts.put("ACC-100", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        accounts.put("ACC-200", new BigDecimal("75000"));
        accounts.put("ACC-300", new BigDecimal("120000"));
        accounts.put("ACC-400", new BigDecimal("200000"));
        accounts.put("ACC-500", new BigDecimal("500000"));

        System.out.println("All accounts: " + accounts);
        System.out.println("First: " + accounts.firstEntry());
        System.out.println("Last: " + accounts.lastEntry());
    }
}
```

```text
All accounts: {ACC-100=50000, ACC-200=75000, ACC-300=120000, ACC-400=200000, ACC-500=500000}
First: ACC-100=50000
Last: ACC-500=500000
```

### How do floor, ceiling, lower, and higher work?

These four methods find the closest match to a given key. The difference is whether the match includes the key itself (floor/ceiling) or excludes it (lower/higher).

| Method | Returns | Includes key itself |
|---|---|---|
| floorKey(k) | Greatest key <= k | Yes |
| ceilingKey(k) | Smallest key >= k | Yes |
| lowerKey(k) | Greatest key < k | No (strictly less) |
| higherKey(k) | Smallest key > k | No (strictly greater) |

Each method also has an Entry variant (floorEntry, ceilingEntry, lowerEntry, higherEntry) that returns the full key-value pair.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.NavigableMap;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class ClosestMatchDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NavigableMap<String, BigDecimal> accounts = new TreeMap<>();
        accounts.put("ACC-100", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        accounts.put("ACC-200", new BigDecimal("75000"));
        accounts.put("ACC-300", new BigDecimal("120000"));
        accounts.put("ACC-400", new BigDecimal("200000"));
        accounts.put("ACC-500", new BigDecimal("500000"));

        String searchKey = "ACC-250";
        System.out.println("Searching for: " + searchKey);
        System.out.println("floorKey   (<=): " + accounts.floorKey(searchKey));
        System.out.println("ceilingKey (>=): " + accounts.ceilingKey(searchKey));
        System.out.println("lowerKey   (<):  " + accounts.lowerKey(searchKey));
        System.out.println("higherKey  (>):  " + accounts.higherKey(searchKey));

        // When the key exists exactly
        String exactKey = "ACC-300";
        System.out.println("\nSearching for exact: " + exactKey);
        System.out.println("floorKey   (<=): " + accounts.floorKey(exactKey));
        System.out.println("lowerKey   (<):  " + accounts.lowerKey(exactKey));
    }
}
```

```text
Searching for: ACC-250
floorKey   (<=): ACC-200
ceilingKey (>=): ACC-300
lowerKey   (<):  ACC-200
higherKey  (>):  ACC-300

Searching for exact: ACC-300
floorKey   (<=): ACC-300
lowerKey   (<):  ACC-200
```

### How do headMap, tailMap, and subMap work?

Range views return a live view of a portion of the map. Changes to the view are reflected in the original map, and changes to the original within the range are reflected in the view.

The one-argument versions use default bounds: headMap(toKey) is exclusive of toKey, tailMap(fromKey) is inclusive of fromKey. The two-argument overloads let you control inclusivity.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.NavigableMap;
import java.util.SortedMap;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class RangeViewDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NavigableMap<String, BigDecimal> accounts = new TreeMap<>();
        accounts.put("ACC-100", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        accounts.put("ACC-200", new BigDecimal("75000"));
        accounts.put("ACC-300", new BigDecimal("120000"));
        accounts.put("ACC-400", new BigDecimal("200000"));
        accounts.put("ACC-500", new BigDecimal("500000"));

        // headMap: all keys < ACC-300 (exclusive by default)
        SortedMap<String, BigDecimal> head = accounts.headMap("ACC-300");
        System.out.println("headMap(<ACC-300): " + head);

        // tailMap: all keys >= ACC-300 (inclusive by default)
        SortedMap<String, BigDecimal> tail = accounts.tailMap("ACC-300");
        System.out.println("tailMap(>=ACC-300): " + tail);

        // subMap with inclusive bounds
        NavigableMap<String, BigDecimal> sub = accounts.subMap(
            "ACC-200", true, "ACC-400", true
        );
        System.out.println("subMap[ACC-200..ACC-400]: " + sub);

        // Views are BACKED by original
        head.put("ACC-050", new BigDecimal("10000"));
        System.out.println("Original after view.put: " + accounts.get("ACC-050"));
    }
}
```

```text
headMap(<ACC-300): {ACC-100=50000, ACC-200=75000}
tailMap(>=ACC-300): {ACC-300=120000, ACC-400=200000, ACC-500=500000}
subMap[ACC-200..ACC-400]: {ACC-200=75000, ACC-300=120000, ACC-400=200000}
Original after view.put: 10000
```

### How do pollFirstEntry and pollLastEntry work?

These methods atomically remove and return the smallest or largest entry. They are useful for priority-based processing where you always consume the next item in order.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class PollEntryDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeMap<BigDecimal, String> pendingTransfers = new TreeMap<>();
        pendingTransfers.put(new BigDecimal("500000"), "TRF-001: Rajesh to Priya");
        pendingTransfers.put(new BigDecimal("10000"), "TRF-002: Priya to savings");
        pendingTransfers.put(new BigDecimal("250000"), "TRF-003: Pankaj salary");

        System.out.println("Processing smallest transfer first:");
        while (!pendingTransfers.isEmpty()) {
            Map.Entry<BigDecimal, String> entry = pendingTransfers.pollFirstEntry();
            System.out.println("  Amount: " + entry.getKey() + " - " + entry.getValue());
        }
        System.out.println("Remaining: " + pendingTransfers.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Processing smallest transfer first:
  Amount: 10000 - TRF-002: Priya to savings
  Amount: 250000 - TRF-003: Pankaj salary
  Amount: 500000 - TRF-001: Rajesh to Priya
Remaining: 0
```

### How is NavigableMap different from SortedMap?

| Feature | SortedMap | NavigableMap |
|---|---|---|
| Closest-match | Not available | floorKey, ceilingKey, lowerKey, higherKey |
| Poll methods | Not available | pollFirstEntry, pollLastEntry |
| Descending view | Not available | descendingMap, descendingKeySet |
| Inclusive bounds | headMap(to) exclusive only | headMap(to, inclusive) |
| Introduced in | Java 2 | Java 6 |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.NavigableMap;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class DescendingViewDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NavigableMap<String, String> accounts = new TreeMap<>();
        accounts.put("ACC-100", "Rajesh");
        accounts.put("ACC-200", "Priya");
        accounts.put("ACC-300", "Pankaj");

        System.out.println("Ascending: " + accounts);
        System.out.println("Descending: " + accounts.descendingMap());
    }
}
```

```text
Ascending: {ACC-100=Rajesh, ACC-200=Priya, ACC-300=Pankaj}
Descending: {ACC-300=Pankaj, ACC-200=Priya, ACC-100=Rajesh}
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Inserting an out-of-range key into a range view

```java
import java.util.*;

// WRONG: adding a key outside the view's range
NavigableMap<String, String> map = new TreeMap<>();
map.put("ACC-200", "Rajesh");
map.put("ACC-300", "Priya");

SortedMap<String, String> head = map.headMap("ACC-300"); // keys < ACC-300
head.put("ACC-400", "Pankaj"); // IllegalArgumentException! ACC-400 >= ACC-300
```

```java
// CORRECT: add to the original map, not the restricted view
map.put("ACC-400", "Pankaj");
System.out.println(map); // {ACC-200=Rajesh, ACC-300=Priya, ACC-400=Pankaj}
```

#### Mistake: Forgetting that headMap(key) is exclusive by default

```java
// WRONG assumption: headMap includes the boundary key
TreeMap<Integer, String> balances = new TreeMap<>();
balances.put(100, "Low");
balances.put(200, "Medium");
balances.put(300, "High");

SortedMap<Integer, String> head = balances.headMap(200);
System.out.println(head); // {100=Low} -- 200 is EXCLUDED
```

```java
// CORRECT: use the two-argument version for inclusive
NavigableMap<Integer, String> headInclusive = balances.headMap(200, true);
System.out.println(headInclusive); // {100=Low, 200=Medium}
```

### What are the edge cases?

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.NavigableMap;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class NavigableEdgeCasesDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NavigableMap<String, String> map = new TreeMap<>();
        map.put("ACC-100", "Rajesh");
        map.put("ACC-300", "Priya");

        // Edge case 1: floor/ceiling when no match exists
        System.out.println("floorKey below all: " + map.floorKey("ACC-050"));
        System.out.println("ceilingKey above all: " + map.ceilingKey("ACC-999"));

        // Edge case 2: pollFirstEntry on empty map
        NavigableMap<String, String> empty = new TreeMap<>();
        System.out.println("pollFirst on empty: " + empty.pollFirstEntry());

        // Edge case 3: subMap where from > to
        try {
            map.subMap("ACC-300", true, "ACC-100", true);
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("from > to: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
floorKey below all: null
ceilingKey above all: null
pollFirst on empty: null
from > to: fromKey > toKey
```

### When should you use NavigableMap?

Use NavigableMap (TreeMap) when you need sorted keys with efficient closest-match lookups, range queries, or priority-based consumption. Common banking scenarios include finding the nearest interest rate tier for a balance, selecting all transactions in a date range, and processing pending transfers in order of amount.

### When should you NOT use NavigableMap?

Do not use TreeMap when you only need key-value lookups by exact key. HashMap provides O(1) average-case performance compared to TreeMap's O(log n). If you do not need sorting or navigation, HashMap is the better choice.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **O(log n) for all operations.** TreeMap uses a red-black tree internally. Every get, put, floor, ceiling, and range-view creation is O(log n).
- **Comparator vs natural ordering.** TreeMap can use a custom Comparator. If no comparator is provided, keys must implement Comparable.
- **ConcurrentSkipListMap.** For concurrent scenarios, use `ConcurrentSkipListMap`, which implements NavigableMap with lock-free reads and O(log n) writes.
- **Range views are O(1) to create** because they are just window objects pointing into the tree. Iterating the view is O(k + log n) where k is the number of elements in the range.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the difference between floorKey and lowerKey?" : floorKey is less than or equal to (inclusive), lowerKey is strictly less than (exclusive).
- "Are headMap/tailMap copies or views?" : Views. They are backed by the original map. Changes propagate both ways.
- "What is the time complexity of TreeMap operations?" : O(log n) for get, put, remove, floor, ceiling, lower, higher.
- "Can you use TreeMap for concurrent access?" : No. Use ConcurrentSkipListMap for a concurrent NavigableMap.
